      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Since $5^2 = 25 > 9 = 3!$. One additional $5$ adds 1 or more zeroes depending on which factor $m$ has. Since factors $11, 11^2, \\dots$, etc. will add atleast 2 more zeroes in addition to the two in $m!$, for a sum of at least 3, its no longer 1992, it has to be below 1992 to then become full tails. To find out whether ${n \\over 9}$ is a multiple of $5$, it is simple enough to check the last digit, if its $5$ or $0$, then if there is at least one power of $5$ in that number, we can easily conclude its a tail number. If it has $2$ or $8$, for example, its not. the tail numbers for taking the powers from $0$ to $4$ is $(5!, 0, 25, 625, 15625)$ so they rest of the powers from various $n$ where $n = 5^0, 5^1, \\dots, 5^4$ follow by the sequence $(n, +5, + 15, + 25, + 35)$, since $n = 1^4$ and not $0$. The theorem is that we add to $5^i$ for ever more parity (odd or even) values of $i$ that result in multiplies of $5$. The extended series then is $(n, +12, + 20, + 31, + 40, + 53, + 61, +72, +84, +99, +110, +127, +140, +153, +171, +190)$ so we have that we can add $5, 12, 20$ to $40$ to reach the most advanced value of $2050$ so at that point it will keep increasing. \nSo having established the terms of ${n \\over 9}$ expanding the term at most gives $1992 \\cdot 400 = 796800$\n$8 \\cdot796800= 6374400$\nthe answer is the difference between ${1992 \\cdot 40}^2-6374400 \\cdot40=6673200$\nSo the answer is: 6673200",
