      "text": "To determine how many positive integers less than 1992 are not factorial tails, we must first find the number of factorial tails less than 1992.\n\nA factorial $m!$ ends with $n$ zeroes, where:\n\\[\nn = \\lfloor\\frac{m}{5}\\rfloor + \\lfloor\\frac{m}{5^2}\\rfloor + \\lfloor\\frac{m}{5^3}\\rfloor + \\dots\n\\]\n\nFirst, observe the case for small values:\n- For $m = 5$, $m! = 5! = 120$ (ends with 1 zero)\n- For $m = 4, 9, 14, 19$ - $m! = 24, 362880, 11409024$, $1625756800$ respectively (ends with 1, 2, 2, 3 zeroes)\n\nNext, we note that for each multiple of five, additional trailing zeroes from successive factorials will arise.  Thus, factorials larger than $5^5=3125$ will surpass $1992$ in length, and contains at least one trailing zero.\n\nNotice recursive structure:\n\n- $5^5$ tails at most $n <= 4$\n- $5^6$ tails at most $n <= 2$\n- $5^7$ tails at most $n <= 1$\n- First, compute factorial tails of each deficiency to find outcome chain\nfor $n \\leq 5 :$\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&5^5: & n &= 0 \\\\ &5^6: & n &= 0 \\\\ &5^7: & n &= 0 \\\\ & 5^8: & n &= 0 \\\\ & 5^9: & n &= 0 \\\\ & 5^{10}: & n &= 0 \\\\ & 5^{11}: & n &= 0 \\\\ & 5^{12}: & n &= 0 \\\\ & 5^{13}: & n &= 0 \\\\ &\n&n \\over 5, \\; n \\over 25, \\; n \\over 125\n& \\bullet \\bullet \\bullet \\\\\n&5^{i+1}: & n &= 0 \\bullet \\bullet \\bullet \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus, for any integer less than $1992$, there exists a corresponding tail.\n\nFinally, determining how many integers create a factorial tail is all but finding a solely one (plus each number above, reducing edges):\n\n$$\n\\boxed {999} \n$$",
